sections.body:
On Linux you parse `ps` output with `awk` because `ps` prints text. PowerShell cmdlets return **objects** — records with named properties — and `Get-Service` returns `System.ServiceProcess.ServiceController` objects. Nothing is text until something displays it.

Ask the object what it has before you filter on it:

```powershell
PS C:\> Get-Service -Name Spooler | Get-Member -MemberType Property

   TypeName: System.ServiceProcess.ServiceController

Name                MemberType Definition
----                ---------- ----------
DependentServices   Property   ...ServiceController[] DependentServices {get;}
DisplayName         Property   string DisplayName {get;set;}
MachineName         Property   string MachineName {get;set;}
ServiceName         Property   string ServiceName {get;}
Status              Property   ...ServiceControllerStatus Status {get;}
```

`Status` is an enum with values such as `Running`, `Stopped`, `StartPending` and `Paused`. `Format-Table` decides how those properties look on screen; `Export-Csv` writes them out as data. Both are display decisions on the same object; only one survives being opened by the next person.
